  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cassio is the lieutenant; Iago is the ancient, and the promotion of Cassio over him is his stated grievance.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Roderigo is in love with Desdemona and funds Iago's scheme, and Iago eventually kills him.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The opening is an alarm in the street, which tells the audience that the marriage will be treated as a public offence.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The delay lets Iago's and Brabantio's language frame Othello before he has a line, and the first speech then corrects it.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    His eloquence is legal evidence in the scene: the Duke is persuaded by a story, which is the play's first lesson in what counts as proof.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line places her love in his mind rather than his body, and the play then destroys exactly that.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Venice is law and report; Cyprus is the frontier where the same man is out of the city's structures, which is why the brawl is possible.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Cassio loses the rank he will spend the play asking to have restored, which makes him the foil to Othello.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The parallel and the difference — recovery is possible for Cassio and impossible for Othello — is the play's argument about honour.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He rarely lies about a fact; he tells Othello what facts mean, and stages the scenes that seem to confirm him.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The repetition hollows the word out, and the audience hears the hollowness every time.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line announces the play's central technique — appearance run against reality — in Iago's own voice.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Emilia's small act of obedience is the mechanism of the tragedy, which is why her role is confidante and witness at once.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    She completes the chain that turns a love token into evidence, and she is a minor figure the paper likes to ask about.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The scene supplies the evidence Othello demands, and the evidence is a misunderstanding he cannot check.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The collapse is the play's clearest evidence, because it happens in front of the audience rather than being reported.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line accepts Iago's premise — that love must be proved — and the rest of the play is the consequence.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Lodovico is the Venetian visitor whose report will carry the story home, so the gesture is also a diplomatic disaster.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Desdemona sings it while preparing for bed, and it was learned from her mother's maid, which is the play's quietest inheritance.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    She takes responsibility for her own death to protect her husband, which is the last and clearest proof of her love.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He smothers her, which is why “put out the light” is both literal and metaphorical.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    She is the witness the play has withheld, and the truth arrives too late to save anyone.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    “*Demand me nothing*” leaves the motive unexplained, which is why motive-hunting is a weak essay strategy.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The administrative machine continues; the dead are not part of the repair.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The speech is a performance of the self he wanted to be, which is why it can be read as both dignity and evasion.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The gap in knowledge is the engine of every ironic line, and it is what makes the audience complicit.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Othello is indispensable to Venice and unassimilable within it, and the play keeps both halves visible.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Her plain speech is never accepted as proof, and the play's tragedy is partly an evidentiary one.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    “How far” wants a proportion and a cost, and the play provides both a self-defence and a body count.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rows give coverage, passages give evidence and claims give arguments; fifty quotations without them is a list.
  ]

]

